#nexus [SplitsTree6]

BEGIN SPLITSTREE6;
DIMENSIONS nDataNodes=13 nAlgorithms=9;
PROGRAM version='SplitsTree App (version 6.5.1-j11, built 01 Oct 2025)';
WORKFLOW creationDate='1759818087852'; [Tue Oct 07 08:21:27 CEST 2025]
END; [SPLITSTREE6]

[
Methods:
Analysis was performed using SplitsTree App 6.0.0 (Huson and Bryant 2024).
The original input consisted of 36 taxa and 36 DNA sequences of length 60.
The P Distance method (Hamming 1950) was used (default options) so as to obtain a 36 x 36 distance matrix.
The Razor Haplo Net method was used (default options, except RemoveEdges=true, ContractEdges=true) so as to obtain 48 nodes and 54 edges.
The Median Joining method (Bandelt et al 1999) was used (default options) so as to obtain 39 nodes and 39 edges.
The Neighbor Net method (Bryant & Moulton 2004, Bryant & Huson 2023) was used (default options) so as to obtain 74 splits, cyclic.
The Show Splits method was used (default options) so as to obtain a Split Network visualization.

References:
Bandelt et al 1999: HJ Bandelt, P. Forster, and A. Röhl. Median-joining networks for inferring intraspecific phylogenies. Molecular Biology and Evolution, 16:37–48, 1999.
Bryant & Huson 2023: D. Bryant and DH Huson, NeighborNet- improved algorithms and implementation. Front. Bioinform. 3, 2023.
Bryant & Moulton 2004: D. Bryant and V. Moulton. Neighbor-net: An agglomerative method for the construction of phylogenetic networks. Molecular Biology and Evolution, 21(2):255– 265, 2004.
Hamming 1950: Hamming, Richard W. Error detecting and error correcting codes. Bell System Technical Journal. 29 (2): 147–160. MR 0035935, 1950.
Huson and Bryant 2024: D.H. Huson and D. Bryant. The SplitsTree App: interactive analysis and visualization using phylogenetic trees and networks. Nature Methods, 2024.
]

